UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Fentanyl-Specific Withdrawal","body":"**Clinical issue:** chronic fentanyl exposure can make buprenorphine initiation less predictable. Time since last use alone is not a reliable marker of receptor-level readiness for buprenorphine.\n\n**Pharmacokinetic considerations:**\n• Fentanyl is highly lipophilic and redistributes into peripheral tissue with repeated exposure\n• Redistribution from tissue may prolong fentanyl/norfentanyl detection after last use\n• In one urine-clearance study of patients with OUD, mean fentanyl clearance was approximately **7.3 days** and norfentanyl clearance was approximately **13.3 days**, with substantial interpatient variability [18]\n• Urine positivity does not prove active intoxication or receptor occupancy, but it supports using clinical withdrawal severity rather than clock time alone\n\n**Mechanism of precipitated withdrawal:**\n• Buprenorphine has high mu-opioid receptor affinity\n• If clinically meaningful full-agonist effect persists, buprenorphine can displace fentanyl or another full agonist from the receptor\n• Because buprenorphine is a partial agonist, abrupt receptor displacement can cause a sudden net reduction in opioid agonism [19][20]\n\n**Risk-reduction approach:**\n1. Do not dose by clock time alone\n2. Do not start standard induction if the patient is sedated, intoxicated, or has only subjective craving/anxiety without objective withdrawal\n3. For fentanyl exposure, use clear objective withdrawal when choosing standard induction. SAMHSA advises adequate withdrawal, COWS >12, and notes fentanyl may require a higher COWS score with lower initial dosing [19]\n4. Prioritize objective signs: mydriasis, piloerection, yawning, rhinorrhea, diarrhea, tachycardia, restlessness\n5. If borderline, consider 2-4 mg SL initially and reassess in 30-60 minutes\n6. If prior precipitated withdrawal, heavy daily fentanyl exposure, inability to tolerate abstinence, or repeated COWS 5-12, use low-dose overlap/microinduction [20]\n7. If moderate/severe withdrawal is clearly established and the patient wants rapid symptom control, ED high-dose buprenorphine remains reasonable with monitoring. ED studies show low overall precipitated-withdrawal rates, but risk varies by setting [9][21][22]\n8. Consider methadone when buprenorphine induction repeatedly fails, the patient prefers methadone, or very high tolerance makes partial-agonist therapy difficult\n\n**If precipitated withdrawal occurs:**\n• Treat symptoms aggressively and maintain engagement\n• Additional buprenorphine is often appropriate, commonly 8-16 mg increments up to 24-32 mg total when clinically indicated\n• Add adjunctive therapy: clonidine, antiemetic, NSAID, fluids, and careful sedation only when needed\n• Reframe as receptor displacement, not allergy or treatment failure\n\n**Bottom line:** fentanyl exposure does not preclude buprenorphine. It requires objective withdrawal assessment, shared induction strategy selection, and readiness to use low-dose overlap when standard induction risk is high.","citation":[9,18,19,20,21,22],"next":"ow-bup-protocol","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"cows","label":"COWS Score"}],"summary":"Fentanyl can persist in tissue; avoid clock-based buprenorphine starts, use objective withdrawal or low-dose overlap.","skippable":true,"safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'ow-fentanyl-nuance' AND tree_id = 'opioid-withdrawal';

